## 1. Testing Overview

This activity focused on testing the Lexer component of the Eternita backend. The purpose was to verify whether the lexer correctly reads policy text and converts it into tokens with the appropriate type, value, line, and column information.

The testing was performed using the existing lexer test file without modifying the backend implementation.

## 2. Testing Environment

The following tools and environment were used:

- Visual Studio Code
- Windows PowerShell
- Node.js
- npm
- Jest testing framework
- TypeScript

**Test File:** `test/policy/lexer.test.ts`  
**Implementation File:** `src/policy/lexer.ts`

## 3. Lexer Test Cases

The existing lexer test file contains five test cases.

| No. | Test Case | Expected Result | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Tokenizes the handout sample policy correctly | Policy text is converted into the expected tokens | PASS |
| 2 | Stores type, value, line, and column for each token | Each token contains the correct information | PASS |
| 3 | Recognizes keywords, names, dates, and numbers | The lexer identifies the expected token types | PASS |
| 4 | Raises an error for an unknown character | A `PolicySyntaxError` is thrown | PASS |
| 5 | Raises an error for an unterminated string | A `PolicySyntaxError` is thrown | PASS |

## 4. Testing Procedure

The following steps were performed during testing:

1. Opened the Eternita repository using Visual Studio Code.
2. Switched to the `backend` branch to test the implementation.
3. Navigated to the `eternita-backend` directory.
4. Located the existing lexer test file at `test/policy/lexer.test.ts`.
5. Executed the lexer test command using PowerShell:

   ```powershell
   npm test -- --runInBand test/policy/lexer.test.ts
   ```

6. Checked the test results to verify whether all lexer test cases passed.
7. Executed the complete backend test suite:

   ```powershell
